# I-CARE 2.1 in BIDS

Continuous ICU EEG from 607 comatose patients after cardiac arrest, converted
from the PhysioNet WFDB release (i-care 2.1, MATLAB v4 .mat + .hea) to BIDS-EEG
with EDF recordings.

## Conversion

Each i-care *segment* (roughly one hour of monitoring) becomes one BIDS run. The
EEG / ECG / REF / OTHER WFDB records of a segment share sampling rate and length
and are merged into a single EDF, with per-channel BIDS types in channels.tsv.

Entities:
  acq-h<HHH>  hour after cardiac arrest at which the segment starts
  run-<NNN>   i-care segment index (unique within a subject; hours are not)

The conversion is numerically lossless: EDF stores the same int16 digital values
as the source .mat, verified by comparing recovered digital samples.

## Units

The source WFDB records declare unit "nu" and the PhysioNet documentation states that
"the recordings do not have units": per-channel amplitude scaling was normalised to the
int16 range, with the scale factor kept in the WFDB gain/baseline fields.

Applying that gain/baseline - exactly as the official PhysioNet Challenge 2023 loader
does - recovers a consistent physical scale. Empirically the result is microvolts:
median per-channel std is ~24, and the five contributing hospitals agree to within a
small factor (13-49). On that basis channels.tsv records units as uV and the EDF
physical dimension is uV, so MNE and other BIDS tooling scale the data correctly.

This is an INFERENCE, not an upstream guarantee. Every recording sidecar carries a
UnitsNote saying so. Treat absolute amplitudes with caution, especially when comparing
across hospitals.

A small number of channels could not have their full int16 span expressed in EDF's
8-character physical-range header field; for those the EDF range covers the actual data
extent instead. They are listed per recording in the sidecar under RescaledChannels.

## Heterogeneity

Sampling rates vary by site (200/250/256/500/512/2048 Hz) as do montages
(19-21 EEG channels, with/without Fpz and F9) and powerline frequency (50/60 Hz).
Sampling rates are 200/250/256/500/512/1024/2048 Hz; they are preserved at their native
value and NOT resampled. Note the cohort spans hospitals A, B, D, E and F - there is no
hospital C in the released data.
Each recording's own values are in its sidecar and channels.tsv.
